The '''History of Guajicutea (1976–2000)''' covers the oil-shock recession that followed the [[Camboriú War]], the collapse of the state hospitality model, and the rise of the [[Partido Unidad y Prosperidad]] (PUP) under [[Julián Mera]]. The period began with rising {{w|unemployment}}, cut flight schedules, and a slump in tourism. It ended with a more stable but more controlled political order, a coast increasingly shaped by private resorts and foreign capital, and a president who remained in power until his death in 2000.

== The oil shock and recession ==

The war and the wider oil disruption pushed global oil prices sharply upward. Guajicutea's airline and cruise operations became far more expensive to run, and many routes were cut. Tourist traffic fell not only in Guajicutea but also in [[Balisca]] and [[Sarta]], where higher transport costs reduced travel volumes across the region. The shock fed directly into the island economy, because travel, hospitality, and port services were among the country's most important employers.

By late 1976, the island had entered a {{w|recession}}. Two of the three major hospitality brands collapsed as demand weakened and operating costs rose. The downturn spread through the transport sector, hotel trade, restaurants, and urban services. What had begun as a war-linked cost spike became a broader economic slump by December 1976.

== Austerity and street unrest ==

The conservative government that followed responded with {{w|austerity}}. Taxes rose, public spending was restrained, and the administration tried to hold the budget together while the tourism sector weakened. The result was politically damaging rather than stabilising. Major cities saw more theft, disorder, and street violence, and protests returned as living conditions worsened.

The baby-boom generation, which had entered the labour market in the late 1960s and early 1970s, now faced a labour shortage in the opposite direction: too many young people, too few jobs. Youth unemployment stayed much higher than the headline rate, especially in the cities. Many young adults drifted into informal work, street gangs, or emigration.

== The rise of the Partido Unidad y Prosperidad ==

Out of the collapse of the old party balance came a new coalition, the [[Partido Unidad y Prosperidad]] (PUP). It brought together breakaway members of the old Liberal and Conservative blocs under [[Julián Mera]], a political operator who presented the new party as a practical alliance between growth-minded modernisers and anti-crisis conservatives. The PUP promised order, jobs, and recovery.

Mera's first years in power were still crisis years. The government kept the state together with an [[Adonian Community]] stabilization loan, tourist subsidies, and the sale of the cruiseline and airline to raise cash. It also passed antinatalist legislation, reflecting the view that the population boom had outpaced jobs and housing.

== Mera's stabilization programme ==

By the early 1980s, Mera had begun to rebuild the economy around a different model. Private foreign investors were encouraged to buy beach land for resort construction, and the state used tax levies to fund an airport and supporting infrastructure. The goal was to rebuild tourism around a more capital-intensive model: fewer mass state services, more private resorts, better connections, and a narrower set of high-value visitors.

The strategy brought construction jobs and revived part of the service economy. It also made the northern coast the center of new growth. But the benefits were uneven. Coastal land values rose, local access tightened, and the gains were concentrated in resort districts and investor networks rather than in the older urban neighborhoods.

Crime had begun to ease by 1992, and tourism slowly returned during the 1990s as oil markets stabilized and air travel recovered. That recovery was real, but it did not reverse the broader social changes of the crisis years. The island was more stable than it had been in the late 1970s, but also more unequal.

== Controlled liberalization ==

Mera remained publicly popular. He was helped by quiet support from the governments of [[Volisania]] and [[Sarta]], both of which saw him as a useful stabilising figure. At the same time, the [[Adonian Community]] periodically called for investigations into election rigging, though those calls did not translate into effective pressure.

The country still had a free press in law, and newspapers remained officially recognised. In practice, however, the largest papers increasingly echoed neoliberal policy lines and tended to support the government’s development agenda. At the same time, personal rights weakened. New laws expanded search-and-seizure powers and found ways around earlier privacy protections. Journalists were arrested, protests were broken up, and unions lost the right to protest independently; labour disputes had to be taken directly to the state.

Mera also tightened control over the judiciary. He removed judges who resisted him and filled the courts with loyalists. That reshaped the legal system from above and made the executive far harder to challenge. The result was a state that still looked constitutional and pluralistic on the surface, but which steadily narrowed the space for dissent.

== Disputed reelection and protests ==

By his later reelection, Mera's opponents accused the government of ballot stuffing and corruption. The vote was widely viewed as manipulated, and the accusations triggered nationwide protests that lasted for a month. Police broke up the demonstrations, and the government held on, but the episode deepened the sense that the political system had become less open and more dependent on force.

The dispute did not end the PUP era, but it fixed its reputation. Supporters saw a government that had prevented collapse and kept the island functioning through crisis. Opponents saw a regime that had traded public accountability for resort development, policing, and controlled elections.

== Public mood ==

The mood of the period shifted from shock to fatigue. The war and oil spike had first produced panic, then recession, then a more general sense that the postwar growth model had failed. Younger Guaji were especially affected: many entered adulthood into a weak labour market, and their political outlook was shaped by {{w|unemployment}}, crime, and a lack of prospects. By the late 1980s and 1990s, the island had stabilised somewhat, but distrust of politics remained high.

